In ASP.NET Core WebAPI, you can retrieve the username (or any other claim) from the JWT token using middleware and DI. Here's an example:

1. Create a custom middleware class that extracts the username claim from the JWT token:

```csharp
public class UserClaimMiddleware
{
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;
    private readonly IConfiguration _config;
    private string _usernameClaimType;

    public UserClaimMiddleware(RequestDelegate next, IConfiguration config)
    {
        _next = next;
        _config = config;
        _usernameClaimType = _config["Jwt:UsernameClaim"];
    }

    public async Task InvokeAsync(HttpContext httpContext)
    {
        // Get the principal from the HTTP context
        var principal = httpContext.User;

        // Check if there is a user claim with the specified type
        var usernameClaim = principal.FindFirst(_usernameClaimType);

        // Set the username property on the HttpContext
        httpContext.Items[typeof(string)] = usernameClaim?.Value;

        // Call the next middleware or controller
        await _next(httpContext);
    }
}
```

2. Configure the middleware in the `Configure()` method of the startup class:

```csharp
services.AddMvc();
services.AddAuthentication(options =>
{
