To convert `argv` (which is an array of null-terminated strings passed as command-line arguments) into a single C string, you can use the following C code:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

char *concatenate_args(int argc, char **argv) {
    int i, len = 0;
    for (i = 0; I < argc; ++i) {
        len += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // Add length of current argument and space between arguments
    }
    char *result = malloc(len);
    for (i = 0, len = 0; I < argc; ++i) {
        strcat(result, argv[i]);
        strcat(result, " ");
    }
    result[len - 1] = '\0'; // Remove trailing space
    return result;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    char *joined = concatenate_args(argc, argv);
    printf("Joined: %s\n", joined);
    free(joined);
    return 0;
}
```

This function `concatenate_args()` takes `argc` and `argv` as input and returns a single string containing all the arguments separated by spaces. It first calculates the total length required and then creates a new string with the necessary memory allocation using `malloc()`. The function then iteratively adds each argument followed by a space using `strcat()`. Finally, it removes the trailing space and returns the resulting string.

