#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#define MAX_ARGUMENTS 1024

char *argv_to_cstring(int argc, char **argv) {
    int i;
    int arg_count = 0;
    char *args_str = malloc(MAX_ARGUMENTS);

    for (i = 0; i < argc; i++) {
        size_t len = strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // add space between arguments and at end

        if (arg_count + len > MAX_ARGUMENTS) {
            // overflow error handling
            free(args_str);
            return NULL;
        }

        strcpy(&args_str[arg_count], argv[i]);
        arg_count += len;

        if (i != argc - 1) { // add space before next argument
            args_str[arg_count++] = ' ';
        }
    }

    // remove trailing space
    args_str[--arg_count] = '\0';
    return args_str;
}

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
    const char *args_str = argv_to_cstring(argc, argv);
    printf("Command-line arguments: %s\n", args_str);
    free(args_str);
    return 0;
}
```

This function takes an array of `argc` and pointers to its corresponding `argv` and returns a single string containing all command-line arguments concatenated with spaces in between. It handles potential buffer overflows by checking the length of each argument and the total length of the resulting string against a maximum limit. The function also removes any trailing space character at the end.

In this implementation, we assume that there will be at most 1024 arguments, which is a reasonable upper bound for typical use cases. If you expect more than that, you may need to adjust the `MAX_ARGUMENTS` definition accordingly.

Here's the C code to convert `argv` (command-line arguments) to a null-terminated string:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

char *join_argv(int argc, char **argv) {
    int size = 0;
    for (int I = 0; I < argc; i++) {
        size += strlen(argv[i]) + 1; // add space between arguments and null terminator
    }
    char *result = malloc(size);
    int pos = 0;
    for (int I = 0; I < argc; i++) {
        strcpy(&result[pos], argv[i]);
        pos += strlen(argv[i]);
        if (i != argc - 1) {
            result[pos++] = ' ';
        }
    }
    result[pos] = '\0';
    return result;
}
```

This function takes an array of arguments (`argv`) and its length (`argc`), concatenates them into a single string (with spaces in between), and returns the resulting string. The function uses dynamic memory allocation with `malloc()` and `free()`. Note that this implementation assumes there's at most one space between adjacent arguments (which is true for typical command lines). If you want to handle multiple spaces or other delimiters, you may need to modify the logic inside the loop. Also note that this implementation has relatively low verbosity (i.e., it's more focused on functionality than readability or style). You can adjust the balance between helpfulness and verbosity according to your preference by adding comments, whitespace, and other formatting choices.

